    {
        "content": "<p>How are you putting a topology on that thing? </p>\n<p>I designed the module topology precisely so that I could put topologies on quaternion algebras over the p-adics. I am not sure I upstreamed enough to mathlib yet but certainly in FLT we have the instance that if A is a module-finite R-algebra for R a commutative topological ring then A with the R-module topology is automatically a topological ring, from which everything else would follow.</p>",
        "id": 579482670,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1773534957
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I see -- the topology is coming from <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Quaternion.instNormedDivisionRingReal#doc\">docs#Quaternion.instNormedDivisionRingReal</a> . There is potential for a diamond here; if R is any topological ring then <code>QuaternionAlegbra R a b c</code> could get a topology making it into a topological ring (the R-module topology), but one probably cannot make this instance without creating a diamond. Usually in these situations I just assume <code>[TopologicalSpace X] [IsModuleTopology R X]</code>.</p>",
        "id": 579482997,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1773535334
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Rather than answering your question, let me ask a question of my own. What do you think <code>Continuous (QuaternionAlgebra.mk (R := ℝ) (a := -1) (b := 0) (c := -1))</code> <em>means</em>? The type of <code>QuaternionAlgebra.mk (R := ℝ) (a := -1) (b := 0) (c := -1)</code> is <code>ℝ → ℝ → ℝ → ℝ → QuaternionAlgebra ℝ (-1) 0 (-1)</code>. Are you sure that you are proving what you want to prove?</p>\n<p>Also worth saying:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">noncomputable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">TopologicalSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">QuaternionAlgebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"bp\">←</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Quaternion</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">infer_instance</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>is probably a terrible idea, because you are constructing data in tactic mode, and <code>rw</code> can generate horrific terms.</p>",
        "id": 579483125,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1773535479
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>For your question, is the issue that <code>ℝ → ℝ → ℝ → ℝ → QuaternionAlgebra ℝ (-1) 0 (-1)</code> is curried so I'm proving something else completely? I'm just trying to prove the standard <code>(a,b,c,d)↦a+bi+cj+dk</code> but I couldn't solve it using simple tactics (on paper I guess I never have to think about this...)</p>\n<p>My understanding of the topology is that it is simply the one induced by the identification with <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{R}^4</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbb\">R</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">4</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>.</p>\n<p>The reason I'm in <code>QuaternionAlgebra</code> is I'm trying to build a quaternion basis using <code>QuaternionAlgebra.Basis</code>, but everything else should be identical to using <code>Quaternion</code>. are you saying even the instance for <code>Quaternion</code> shouldn't be there?</p>",
        "id": 579484175,
        "sender_full_name": "ZhiKai Pong",
        "timestamp": 1773536429
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes, you're proving something about a map from the reals to an uncountable product of copies of the quaternions with the product topology or equivalently that for all b,c,d the map sending a to a+bi+cj+dk is a continuous map from R to H. </p>\n<p>If you are only interested in H I don't really understand why you're using QuaternionAlgebra at all; it looks like we already have API specifically for the Hamilton quaternions.</p>",
        "id": 579493036,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1773538792
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thank you, I didn't realise what QuaternionAlgebra was doing and certainly wasn't aware of it's use outside of the standard Hamilton usage which got me into a lot of nonsense. I refactored my construction and everything seems to work now.</p>",
        "id": 579546745,
        "sender_full_name": "ZhiKai Pong",
        "timestamp": 1773579768
    }
]